    "longDescription": "Subresource Integrity is a distinct capability within Security Engineering. Pin expected hashes for external static resources where feasible. In this Foundry it is treated as a composable building block rather than a checkbox: the implementation should define what data it owns, which events or APIs it consumes and emits, what permissions it needs, how users can see its state, and what happens when it is unavailable. The primary architectural objective is least-privilege protection and verifiable integrity.\n\nA production implementation should avoid coupling Subresource Integrity directly to unrelated UI or transport code. Prefer a small adapter or service boundary, versioned inputs and outputs, feature detection where browser support varies, explicit initialization and cleanup, and observable error states. For online multiplayer systems, decide whether the capability is local-only, replicated peer state, authoritative server state, or derived/cache state. For AI-assisted systems, label whether generated material is advisory, draft, reviewed or verified before it can change consequential shared state.\n\nRecommended contexts include public web apps, institutional deployments, plugin systems, AI-assisted applications. The feature should remain portable: project data should survive implementation replacement where practical, and a user should be able to understand why the feature exists, what it does, and what trade-offs it introduces.",
