    "tooltip": "Recover from a bad configuration update. Best used as part of resilience & graceful degradation. Implementation: clear contracts, capability detection, versioning, observability and documented fallback behavior. Main risks: hidden coupling, unsupported assumptions, stale state and difficult recovery. Fallback: degrade to a simpler explicit workflow while preserving core data and user agency.",
    "longDescription": "Last-known-good config is a distinct capability within Resilience & Graceful Degradation. Recover from a bad configuration update. In this Foundry it is treated as a composable building block rather than a checkbox: the implementation should define what data it owns, which events or APIs it consumes and emits, what permissions it needs, how users can see its state, and what happens when it is unavailable. The primary architectural objective is resilience & graceful degradation.\n\nA production implementation should avoid coupling Last-known-good config directly to unrelated UI or transport code. Prefer a small adapter or service boundary, versioned inputs and outputs, feature detection where browser support varies, explicit initialization and cleanup, and observable error states. For online multiplayer systems, decide whether the capability is local-only, replicated peer state, authoritative server state, or derived/cache state. For AI-assisted systems, label whether generated material is advisory, draft, reviewed or verified before it can change consequential shared state.\n\nRecommended contexts include mission-critical tools, field work, large PWAs, public services. The feature should remain portable: project data should survive implementation replacement where practical, and a user should be able to understand why the feature exists, what it does, and what trade-offs it introduces.",
    "implementation": "Implementation pattern: clear contracts, capability detection, versioning, observability and documented fallback behavior. Start with a typed or schema-validated interface for Last-known-good config; define initialization, cleanup, timeout/cancellation behavior, permissions and telemetry/privacy boundaries; then add tests for normal operation and failure. Keep UI state separate from durable domain state. If the capability participates in multiplayer, define ordering, idempotency, replay and reconnect behavior. If it processes external or user-supplied content, validate at the trust boundary and render untrusted content safely.",
    "fusion": "Fusion guidance: connect Last-known-good config through the shared route, capability, event and schema registries instead of calling other modules ad hoc. It normally gains value when paired with persistence, provenance, accessibility, diagnostics and an explicit fallback. In a feature-fusion prompt, ask the coding model to name the events shared with adjacent modules, the source of truth for each piece of state, and the condition under which this feature should deactivate or downgrade.",
    "risks": "Failure modes to design for include hidden coupling, unsupported assumptions, stale state and difficult recovery. Also test unsupported browsers, interrupted initialization, stale versions, malformed inputs, permission denial, offline transitions and partial upgrades. Do not present availability, security, correctness or impact claims as proven unless the relevant checks were actually performed.",
    "fallback": "Graceful fallback: degrade to a simpler explicit workflow while preserving core data and user agency. Preserve readable/exportable state whenever possible, explain the degraded mode in plain language, and never make an optional advanced capability a single point of failure for the whole workspace."
