    "longDescription": "AI confidence/evidence indicators is a distinct capability within Local & Hybrid AI. Expose evidence coverage and uncertainty cues without pretending model confidence is calibrated truth. In this Foundry it is treated as a composable building block rather than a checkbox: the implementation should define what data it owns, which events or APIs it consumes and emits, what permissions it needs, how users can see its state, and what happens when it is unavailable. The primary architectural objective is bounded local or hybrid intelligence.\n\nA production implementation should avoid coupling AI confidence/evidence indicators directly to unrelated UI or transport code. Prefer a small adapter or service boundary, versioned inputs and outputs, feature detection where browser support varies, explicit initialization and cleanup, and observable error states. For online multiplayer systems, decide whether the capability is local-only, replicated peer state, authoritative server state, or derived/cache state. For AI-assisted systems, label whether generated material is advisory, draft, reviewed or verified before it can change consequential shared state.\n\nRecommended contexts include private copilots, prompt improvement, research synthesis, agent-assisted workflows. The feature should remain portable: project data should survive implementation replacement where practical, and a user should be able to understand why the feature exists, what it does, and what trade-offs it introduces.",
    "implementation": "Implementation pattern: model capability detection, structured inputs/outputs, provenance, tool scopes, privacy controls and evaluations. Start with a typed or schema-validated interface for AI confidence/evidence indicators; define initialization, cleanup, timeout/cancellation behavior, permissions and telemetry/privacy boundaries; then add tests for normal operation and failure. Keep UI state separate from durable domain state. If the capability participates in multiplayer, define ordering, idempotency, replay and reconnect behavior. If it processes external or user-supplied content, validate at the trust boundary and render untrusted content safely.",
    "fusion": "Fusion guidance: connect AI confidence/evidence indicators through the shared route, capability, event and schema registries instead of calling other modules ad hoc. It normally gains value when paired with persistence, provenance, accessibility, diagnostics and an explicit fallback. In a feature-fusion prompt, ask the coding model to name the events shared with adjacent modules, the source of truth for each piece of state, and the condition under which this feature should deactivate or downgrade.",
    "risks": "Failure modes to design for include hallucination, prompt injection, excessive cost, hidden data transfer and hardware incompatibility. Also test unsupported browsers, interrupted initialization, stale versions, malformed inputs, permission denial, offline transitions and partial upgrades. Do not present availability, security, correctness or impact claims as proven unless the relevant checks were actually performed.",
    "fallback": "Graceful fallback: retain deterministic non-AI workflows and allow explicit remote fallback only with user consent. Preserve readable/exportable state whenever possible, explain the degraded mode in plain language, and never make an optional advanced capability a single point of failure for the whole workspace."
