    "implementation": "Implementation pattern: threat modeling, strict input/output boundaries, secure defaults, key lifecycle and browser security policy. Start with a typed or schema-validated interface for Permission dashboard; define initialization, cleanup, timeout/cancellation behavior, permissions and telemetry/privacy boundaries; then add tests for normal operation and failure. Keep UI state separate from durable domain state. If the capability participates in multiplayer, define ordering, idempotency, replay and reconnect behavior. If it processes external or user-supplied content, validate at the trust boundary and render untrusted content safely.",
    "fusion": "Fusion guidance: connect Permission dashboard through the shared route, capability, event and schema registries instead of calling other modules ad hoc. It normally gains value when paired with persistence, provenance, accessibility, diagnostics and an explicit fallback. In a feature-fusion prompt, ask the coding model to name the events shared with adjacent modules, the source of truth for each piece of state, and the condition under which this feature should deactivate or downgrade.",
    "risks": "Failure modes to design for include XSS, privilege escalation, unsafe imports, secret leakage and false assurance. Also test unsupported browsers, interrupted initialization, stale versions, malformed inputs, permission denial, offline transitions and partial upgrades. Do not present availability, security, correctness or impact claims as proven unless the relevant checks were actually performed.",
    "fallback": "Graceful fallback: disable optional capability, preserve read-only access and surface recovery guidance. Preserve readable/exportable state whenever possible, explain the degraded mode in plain language, and never make an optional advanced capability a single point of failure for the whole workspace."
