    "maturity": "stable",
    "tooltip": "Lock in fixes for previously discovered input, authorization or rendering vulnerabilities. Best used as part of evidence that implementations behave as intended. Implementation: deterministic fixtures, automated assertions, failure simulation and clear distinction between tested and untested behavior. Main risks: false confidence from shallow tests, flaky suites and untested fallbacks. Fallback: ship self-tests and manual verification guidance when full automation is unavailable.",
    "longDescription": "Security regression tests is a distinct capability within Testing & Self-Verification. Lock in fixes for previously discovered input, authorization or rendering vulnerabilities. In this Foundry it is treated as a composable building block rather than a checkbox: the implementation should define what data it owns, which events or APIs it consumes and emits, what permissions it needs, how users can see its state, and what happens when it is unavailable. The primary architectural objective is evidence that implementations behave as intended.\n\nA production implementation should avoid coupling Security regression tests directly to unrelated UI or transport code. Prefer a small adapter or service boundary, versioned inputs and outputs, feature detection where browser support varies, explicit initialization and cleanup, and observable error states. For online multiplayer systems, decide whether the capability is local-only, replicated peer state, authoritative server state, or derived/cache state. For AI-assisted systems, label whether generated material is advisory, draft, reviewed or verified before it can change consequential shared state.\n\nRecommended contexts include long-lived software, offline apps, multiplayer systems, public releases. The feature should remain portable: project data should survive implementation replacement where practical, and a user should be able to understand why the feature exists, what it does, and what trade-offs it introduces.",
    "implementation": "Implementation pattern: deterministic fixtures, automated assertions, failure simulation and clear distinction between tested and untested behavior. Start with a typed or schema-validated interface for Security regression tests; define initialization, cleanup, timeout/cancellation behavior, permissions and telemetry/privacy boundaries; then add tests for normal operation and failure. Keep UI state separate from durable domain state. If the capability participates in multiplayer, define ordering, idempotency, replay and reconnect behavior. If it processes external or user-supplied content, validate at the trust boundary and render untrusted content safely.",
