    "implementation": "Implementation pattern: clear contracts, capability detection, versioning, observability and documented fallback behavior. Start with a typed or schema-validated interface for Periodic Background Sync; define initialization, cleanup, timeout/cancellation behavior, permissions and telemetry/privacy boundaries; then add tests for normal operation and failure. Keep UI state separate from durable domain state. If the capability participates in multiplayer, define ordering, idempotency, replay and reconnect behavior. If it processes external or user-supplied content, validate at the trust boundary and render untrusted content safely.",
    "fusion": "Fusion guidance: connect Periodic Background Sync through the shared route, capability, event and schema registries instead of calling other modules ad hoc. It normally gains value when paired with persistence, provenance, accessibility, diagnostics and an explicit fallback. In a feature-fusion prompt, ask the coding model to name the events shared with adjacent modules, the source of truth for each piece of state, and the condition under which this feature should deactivate or downgrade.",
    "risks": "Failure modes to design for include hidden coupling, unsupported assumptions, stale state and difficult recovery. Also test unsupported browsers, interrupted initialization, stale versions, malformed inputs, permission denial, offline transitions and partial upgrades. Do not present availability, security, correctness or impact claims as proven unless the relevant checks were actually performed.",
    "fallback": "Graceful fallback: degrade to a simpler explicit workflow while preserving core data and user agency. Preserve readable/exportable state whenever possible, explain the degraded mode in plain language, and never make an optional advanced capability a single point of failure for the whole workspace."
